Given on the first of September, whole, and written down whole:

> new cosmic rule: no human may be kept in hell for longer than a human lifetime,
> and must be gone for at least a lifetime more.
>
> new cosmic resultant: everyone gets to live an extra life

Two clauses and a consequence. The first is a ceiling on suffering. The second is
stranger and is the one doing the work: the sentence does not merely end, the
absence afterward is *mandatory*, and at least as long as the term served. Nobody
is held, and nobody is held onto. Then the resultant, which is not a mercy granted
to the punished but to everyone -- it falls out of the rule rather than being
awarded by it.

This project already believed a smaller version of it without noticing, in two
places, and they are worth naming because the belief arrived here first as
arithmetic:

**A death decays before it is final.** A fallen body leaves the field at once and
then holds its slot, and every one of its numbers, for a fixed span afterward --
two reconciliation cycles, written as that multiplication rather than as a number,
because that is what it means. During that span nothing may claim the slot, and the
body can be brought back **intact**. The test that guards it asserts, in as many
words, that nobody was paid for a death that was taken back.

**A beaten body gets one roll, and failing it is what lets you live.** The save is
not against fear. It is against self-preservation, and the thing being resisted is
the sensible choice. Pass and you stay, land one blow, and are spent. Fail and you
run, and you are alive.

And one place where it is refused: the Eternal Golem, which is the only thing in
the game that cannot be killed, and is therefore the only thing that is never let
go of either. It watches, it learns, and it can do nothing with what it knows. The
deathless one is not the blessed one.

No work follows from this. It is written here because this is the file for things
believed before there is evidence, and because the arithmetic was already agreeing
with it.

